Now the test checks `getByRole('list')` — verifying the `<ul>` exists when expanded. No class name coupling. 

Empty-state tests verify non-collapsibility by asserting `queryByRole('button')` returns null — no toggle button means no collapsing.

---

## File 10 of 12: `frontend/__tests__/contract-results-page.test.tsx`

Integration-level tests for the full results page. Mocks context and navigation, tests the page as a whole.

**PR excerpt:**

```typescript
describe('ContractPage', () => {
  beforeEach(() => {
    vi.resetModules();
    mockGetResult.mockReset();
  });

  it('renders score badge with correct label', async () => {
    mockGetResult.mockReturnValue(SUCCESS_RESULT);
    const { default: ContractPage } = await import('@/app/contract/[id]/page');
    render(<ContractPage />);
    expect(screen.getByText('Egregious')).toBeInTheDocument();
  });

  it('renders summary text', async () => { /* ... */ });
  it('renders link back to evaluate page', async () => { /* ... */ });
  it('renders fallback when no result found', async () => { /* ... */ });

  it('renders three sections in order: Egregious, Unfair, Fair', async () => {
    mockGetResult.mockReturnValue(SUCCESS_RESULT);
    const { default: ContractPage } = await import('@/app/contract/[id]/page');
    render(<ContractPage />);
    const buttons = screen.getAllByRole('button');
    expect(buttons[0]).toHaveTextContent(/Egregious/);
    expect(buttons[1]).toHaveTextContent(/Unfair/);
    expect(buttons[2]).toHaveTextContent(/Fair/);
  });

  it('shows correct clause count per section', async () => { /* ... */ });

  it('shows placeholders for empty tiers', async () => {
    const fairOnlyResult: EvalSuccess = {
      ...SUCCESS_RESULT,
      overall_fairness: 'fair',
      clauses: [SUCCESS_RESULT.clauses[2]],
    };
    mockGetResult.mockReturnValue(fairOnlyResult);
    const { default: ContractPage } = await import('@/app/contract/[id]/page');
    render(<ContractPage />);
    expect(screen.getByText(/No egregious clauses/)).toBeInTheDocument();
    expect(screen.getByText(/No unfair clauses/)).toBeInTheDocument();
  });
});
```

Walk through: why does every test use dynamic `await import()` with `vi.resetModules()` in `beforeEach`, and what does the "shows placeholders for empty tiers" test verify that the unit tests don't? Restate in your own words.

---

## File 11 of 12: `frontend/__tests__/loading-shimmer.test.tsx`

Updated to reflect the flicker fix. The old "hides shimmer when response arrives" test is replaced with two tests: one confirming shimmer *stays* on success, one confirming it hides on error.

**PR excerpt (the changed tests):**

```typescript
  test('keeps shimmer on success until navigation', async () => {
    await stageFileAndSubmit();
    expect(screen.getByTestId('loading-shimmer')).toBeInTheDocument();

    resolveFetch();

    await waitFor(() => {
      expect(screen.getByTestId('loading-shimmer')).toBeInTheDocument();
    });
  });

  test('hides shimmer on error', async () => {
    await stageFileAndSubmit();
    expect(screen.getByTestId('loading-shimmer')).toBeInTheDocument();

    fetchResolver(new Response(JSON.stringify({ error: true, reason: 'fail' }), { status: 200 }));

    await waitFor(() => {
      expect(screen.queryByTestId('loading-shimmer')).not.toBeInTheDocument();
    });
  });
```

Walk through: why do we need *two* tests now instead of the original one, and how does the error test simulate a failed evaluation? Restate in your own words.

